  <!-- ════════ BOTH-SIDES CALLOUT ════════ -->
  <section class="crp-section" id="both-sides" aria-label="The other side">
    <div class="crp-bothsides">
      <div class="crp-bothsides-tag">The other side of this</div>
      <p>A comparison is only as honest as the world both plans share. Everything above assumes returns arrive in trend order &mdash; smoothly, on schedule, every year &mdash; and real markets do not deliver an average annually. The bear toggle puts one crash in both plans, which answers whether an advantage survives a hostile market; it does not tell you whether the plan itself survives, because it varies nothing. That is the <a href="/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test">Retirement Stress Test</a>&rsquo;s job, and it is the next question after this one.</p>
      <p>The deeper caution is the model. Under the Power Law the trend growth rate declines with time, so the value of retiring one year later is not a constant &mdash; it is worth more at some dates than others, and a difference that looks decisive at one retirement year can shrink to noise at another. Move both plans out a decade and read the table again before you treat any margin here as settled.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
